  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nBroad upper ridge remains across the southern US. Closer to the \nsurface, a 30-40 knot low level jet remains from eastern TX into \ncentral Indiana.  This low level jet has prevented the atmosphere \nfrom cooling supstantially, and fueled storms across the region. \nThese storms are handled relatively poorly by various models, but \nhave begun to be recognized by the HRRR/RAP over the last few hours. \n Expect the threat elevated convection to remain through the mid \nmorning hours but expect an overall weakening trend as the morning \ngoes on.  There is a shortwave across south central Nebraska as of \n08Z, and with ample mid level moisture, could see a renewed threat \nof elevated showers/thunderstorms across northern Missouri mid-day. \nWith the expected cloud cover and showers, expect high temperatures \nto be moderated a bit.  Considered a heat advisory along and south \nof I-70 (north of the excessive heat warning), but with impacts of \ncloud cover and showers, opted to just stick with the excessive heat \nwarning across central MO.  \n\nCAMs suggest convection redeveloping across the High Plains late \nthis afternoon, pushing east/southeast tonight on the leading edge \nof the thermal ridge. With this activitiy once again building into a \nstregthening 40-50 knot low level jet, could see these storms last \nlonger than CAMs suggest leading to another night of potential \nscattered storms. \n\nClearing skies through the day is once again expected to lead to \nanoother mild day with highs in the mid and upper 90s with heat \nindices of 105-110 F degrees.  Will need to once again consider a \nheat advisory for Tuesday depending on potential clearing.  \n\nStationary front across Central Iowa along I-80 may try to work \nsouth as low pressure develops in western KS and gradually pushes \neast.  South of the front, warm and humid conditions are expected to \ncontinue, so location of the front will be key.  Low level jet south \nof the boundary will likely fuel nocturnal thunderstorms on the \nnorth side of boundary Wednesday night. Additional ridge riding \nshortwaves are expected through the late week into the weekend \nleading for additional periodic chances for showers and \nthunderstorms.",
